==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|eschete}}, from {{etyl|xno|en}} {{m|fro|escheat}}, {{etyl|fro|en}} {{m|fro|eschet}}, {{m|fro|escheit}}, {{m|fro|escheoit||that which falls to one}}, from the past participle of {{m|fro|escheoir||to fall}}, from {{etyl|VL.|en}} {{m|la|*excadō}}, from {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|ex}} + {{m|la|cadō||I fall}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/əˈst͡ʃit/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|legal}} The [[return]] of [[property]] of a [[deceased]] person to the [[state]] (originally to a [[feudal]] [[lord]]) where there are no [[legal]] [[heir]]s or [[claimant]]s.
# {{lb|en|legal}} The property so [[revert]]ed.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[plunder|Plunder]], [[booty]].
#* '''1590''', Edmund Spenser, ''The Faerie Queene'', III.viii:
#*: Approching, with bold words and bitter threat, / Bad that same boaster, as he mote, on high / To leaue to him that Lady for '''excheat''', / Or bide him battell without further treat.
# That which falls to one; a reversion or return.
#* Spenser
#*: To make me great by others' loss is bad '''escheat'''.

====Quotations====
* {{seeCites}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|of property}} To revert to a [[state]] or [[lord]] because its previous owner died without an heir.

